Date: Thu, 11 Feb 1993 00:48:15 -0800
From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: VR: Topologies of virtual worlds

"It was a large room.  Full of people.  . . .  And they were all free.  And
they were all asking themselves the same question:  What is behind that
curtain?"  (Laurie Anderson)

And why is the Metaverse, in Neal Stephenson's novel "Snow Crash", a sphere?

Probably because no one likely to read the novel needs to have the concept
of a sphere explained.  A sphere has disadvantages, though.  To simulate
movement on a sphere, you have to do spherical trigonometry, which involves
transcendental functions, where simple vector arithmetic suffices for
motions in a Euclidean space where gravity is always in the same direction.
(Think of the trig that some computer had to do to handle Hiro's battle on
wheels with Raven.)

Perhaps the Metaverse was made round just for the opportunity to use fancy
geometry hacks.  Perhaps just because it's _not_ the flat torus we all
expect from games like Lunatic Fringe.  (A "torus" here means a rectangle
whose opposite edges wrap around.  Plug: If you have a color Mac, get "More
After Dark".)  Or -- a practical reason at last -- perhaps to provide a
horizon, to save the chore of a hidden-planes analysis on every building in
the Metaverse.

Wander with me beyond Mr Stephenson's featureless black globe.

(I dismiss Mr Gibson's cyberspace, which seems to be a parallel universe --
found, not created, by Man -- whose geography matches our own.  It never
held water for me.  Before Gibson there was Vinge's "True Names", whose
Other Plane is mostly in the mind of the user.  The glimpses we get of its
structure are interesting, but nothing solid enough to hook these musings
onto.)

- - -

What are the necessary features of a Virtual Universe?

First, it must satisfy the sense(s) by which its users navigate.  For us,
that means sight.  Objects in the VU must be visisble.  The VU must have a
visible structure, which may be as simple as gridlines or as rich as a full-
scale map of Renaissance Italy.  The user should never have a sense of being
adrift in the Void.  The geometry of the VU must be locally consistent with
that of the user's home universe.  (This could be a problem if we ever get a
Net hookup with a sharply curved six-space.:)

Second, pieces of the VU must flow smoothly into each other at their
boundaries.  The system must have transparent protocols to allow a user to
walk from a space generated by one host machine to a space generated by
another.  Also, the boundaries should be visually transparent: on
approaching one, you should see no black curtain, but whatever scenery is
visible to one standing a yard beyond the boundary.

Third, if it is to be used by people accustomed to walking on a plane, the
VU should provide such a plane.  This plane need not have global planar
topology, and I'm coming to that.  The plane may also be hilly.

Fourth, the VU ought to be able to grow.

- - -

I imagine a Virtual Universe growing by accretion of polygons.  Two hosts
agree to generate a VU in which each rules an equilateral triangle (or
rather a triangular column of space), and the two triangles meet along one
edge.   (I think squares or hexagons more likely, but my examples will use
triangles for greater flexibility, if you'll pardon the pun.)  A ray traced
from inside one triangle goes through the other and, if not obstructed, out
one of its far edges.  By and by, more hosts attach their own triangles to
the free edges.  (I keep thinking of Michael Moorcock's prologue to one of
the "Elric" books, in which a Hero comes to the Tower at the Edge of the
World, and following the instructions of the Woman he meets there, impresses
his will on the Sea of Chaos, to extend the World by a few feet or miles.
He dies, by the way, like all the rest; but our Sea of Chaos will not be so
formidable.)

If at each vertex six triangles meet, the resulting structure is a plane,
potentially infinite.  If no vertex has more than six, but some have fewer,
the structure must curve back on itself; if exactly five triangles meet at
each vertex, there can be only twenty triangles in this universe.  (Put
another way, the total angular deficit at the vertices can be no more than 4
pi, or twelve triangles.)

What if more than six triangles are allowed to meet at a vertex?  Observe
that nothing in the above definition forbids it.  Rays of light need not
interact with the vertices, which are infinite columns infinitely thin.  If
geometry is Euclidean, then a vertex where seven triangles meet looks like a
discontinuity:  a slice out of the action.  You can't walk through such a
vertex; you'd be sliced in two, your segments proceeding on paths sixty
degrees apart.

And if geometry is not Euclidean?  Suppose the protocols for light (and
objects) passing through the boundaries around this vertex include a sort of
refraction, so that the discontinuity goes away.  (I regret that I lack the
math for this; can someone recommend a book that covers non-Euclidean
analytic geometry?)  Now you can walk through the vertex without being
sliced, though you might be stretched or squeezed.

If no vertex has *fewer* than six faces, space is negatively curved.  It is
infinite, and roomier than a flat space, in the sense that a given radius
encompasses a bigger area.  If you tried to make a solid model of this
seven-for-six plane, to scale, it would resemble the petals of certain
flowers.  Being Out of Touch with Nature, I don't know the name of the
flower I have in mind, but I'll bet if you look at its petals under a
microscope you'll see a regular lattice of cells with lots of
discontinuities, where these extra pie-wedges are introduced.

And if the number of triangles around a particular vertex is infinite?  Then
you've got a singularity in a Riemann surface, which no tricks with
refraction can cure.  Consider a helical parking garage:  if you walk a full
circle around the axis, you find yourself above or below where you started.
This singularity is like that, but without the slope.  Walk a full circle
around it and you're . . . just elsewhere, that's all.

- - -

But perhaps, as Mr Stephenson postulates, the Virtual Universe's geometry
will be fixed at its creation, and finite.  (Mr Stephenson mentions once, by
the way, that there are private tracts of the Metaverse, away from the
Street, where the consensus geometry is not in effect.)  So what happens
when the world (or, more literally, its address-space) is full?  Do we
resign ourselves to limits?  Surely not.

To expand a torus while maintaining its overall geometry, slice it (parallel
to one of its edges) and insert new flooring to fill the gap.  For a sphere,
any slice -- with Hiro's magic katana? -- will do.  The best place to slice,
though, is down the middle of Great Circle Street (which becomes two
streets).  The space between should belong to the lots fronting the Street,
to compensate their owners for the disruption to their neighborhood.

On a sphere, God now must choose: preserve local geometry, or overall
geometry?  Initially, the new land is a cylinder between two spherical caps;
if future slices are not parallel to the first, geometry gets rather ugly.
If overall geometry is to be preserved, there must be an earthquake,
deforming the surface into a new bigger sphere.  And here another choice
must be made:  Equal-area or conformal?  In fitting a sphere to a bigger
sphere (or a plane), you can preserve area, or you can preserve shape; not
both.  I incline to the conformal.  (In practice, if you added a mile to
Hiro's Metaverse, I think hardly anyone would notice the distortion.)

Two other ways to expand:  magic stairwells and magic gateways.

A "magic stairwell" is a tower whose top is hidden in clouds.  It's also a
well.  Go up the tower or down the well, and find yourself emerging from a
well or a tower (respectively) in another plane, parallel to the first.  To
keep the mental map consistent, the magic stairwells up and down from a
given plane lead to the same two adjacent planes, where they maintain their
relative positions.

A "magic gateway" is a finite boundary along which one universe is exchanged
for another.  When your left foot is leaving the front of one gateway, your
right foot is landing in the back of its mate; meanwhile someone else can be
stepping through on the other side.  (If you go twice around either
doorpost, you make one complete circuit.)  A wide gateway can replace an
edge of a torus -- enough of those, and the topology of the Virtual
Multiverse would give anyone a headache.

- - -

Some of us as uploads will dwell in such universes, and invite our neighbors
in for virtual barbecues.  How shall the world be furnished?

I think there's no excuse not to have hills and seas.  Even I can generate
fractal terrain (I've done it).  Forests, streams and meadows would be nice,
too.

And why not a sort of geology?  I have in mind a series of long-period
ripples in the "crust" of the world, such that in the course of a few years
any given point is likely to be mountaintop and undersea.  The ripples
should be chaotic enough that the map practically never repeats.  With luck,
water will be caught and lifted into mountain lakes, and leak out again as
ephemeral rivers.  (This might be easier than convincingly simulating
rainfall.)  Buildings in such a world might be designed as clusters of
towers, such that the foot of each tower "floats" independently to stay on
the ground, and the configuration of stairs between adjacent towers varies
with the underlying slope.  When my residence is submerged, I grow fins.

Astronomy?  Just once, Mr Stephenson tells us that there are stars over the
Metaverse -- Y.T. uses them to measure Rife's black cube at the Antipodes.
What sort of stars are they?  Random twinkles -- a one-bit dither of very
dark gray?  Or a map, installed and improved by hackers, of the sky over
Earth?  (The Street represents the Equator, and Port Zero marks the Prime
Meridian.)  With radio sources painted as red clouds, xray sources as
brilliant blue points, and pulsars as winking green eyes?

The sky over a torus, unfortunately, is as tamely periodic as any tile
floor, and cannot be mapped to a sphere.

If the Virtual World is a sphere, there ought to be other spheres -- at
least one sun, at least one moon.  If the World is a playground rather than
a commercial zone, perhaps the planets collide, splash, re-form
(miraculously preserving surfaces).  Each planet is populated, of course,
with Biomorphs (see Dawkins, "The Blind Watchmaker"), and at collisions they
cross-pollinate.

