From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Misc.

Tom's been busy.  Some responses to some of his claims: the rarity of
smokers breathing into baby's faces does little to refute that this is an
assault: my friend Cris started smoking in precisely that manner.

But this whole question has gotten confused on the matter of "assault,"
a criminal charge.  The real question isn't whether the costs smokers
externalize onto others are criminal, but whether they're torts, civil
offenses.  Under Richard Epstein's theory of strict liability, it would
seem that all such accidentally externalized costs would be torts, and
those the costs are imposed upon would have just claims to legal remedy
in the form of restitution at least (they might have claims to injunctive
relief).

So, if smokers harm others, then those others oughta be able to sue for
restitution and get smokers ordered by court of law to stop harming them.

The big question here is whether any harm is inflicted, and, if so, how
much?  Furthermore, if there is harm, then why doesn't anyone sue already?
If there aren't any obstacles to such a suit, then surely such a litigious
society as the American Empire would have done so!  Perhaps the grounds
for proving harm are too flimsy.

Now to attack Tom for his claims about collective justification.  Assume
that A accepts statism, and B doesn't.  A supports coercion, and B doesn't.
A supports coercion of B, and B doesn't support coercion of A.  Surely
there's something wrong with saying that coercion of B is justified
relative to A, but coercion of A isn't relative to B.  I think Tom is
confusing belief with truth here.  A believes that coercion of B is
just, but that doesn't make it so.  Correspondence to reality does.

Now, what else?  Ah, yes.  Contrary to someone's claim, police brutality
isn't universal, because police aren't universal.  England didn't have
any until Sir Robert Peel invented them in the early 1800s (they were
originally called Peelers).  The North American Continent didn't have any
until the 1840s (where they were originally established to fight rioters).
Police don't seem to exist at all since 6,000 years ago (the dawn of
human history) until about 200 years ago - and those were the secret
police forces of the Russian Empire and the French Kingdom.

Contrary to Perry's claim, I've been suspecting for a while that the true
perversion is the use of police to fight crime.  It seems that the true
purpose of monopolizing law-enforcement is entirely statist.

When was the last time you heard about private security agency brutality?
I've never heard of it.

Oh, and before I forget, I think that the children should be the ones to
sue their parents and anyone else who harms them, too.

Tim May's time-release cryptography proposal seems like it would be useful
for proving priority of discovery or invention of scientific or technical
nature.  Perhaps this will help defenders of claims to intellectual
property, even though Tim May claims not to believe in such claims,
ironically!  "I can prove I invented it first.  I've had the information
sealed in crypto-escrow since..."

Let's see, anything else?  I'm sure I'll think of something else later...

