Date: Thu, 11 Feb 93 9:44:19 EST
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: PHIL/META A Manifesto for newbies

While I was driving in this a.m., I had an idea that may be useful
for dealing with questions from newbies and prevent some repetitve
debates.

Why not create what is essentially a manifesto with more concrete
proposals than is in the intro statement/FAQ. Some of the problems
come from not the principles (which most agree on), but the
application.

Here is a sample of what I would put in it.

Since excessive government regulation creates higher prices and
stifles innovation, we propose to eliminate the FDA etc. that
regulate and test products. In its place private companies would
exist that examine products and rate them as safe or unsafe.
This would take two forms. One, (acting like a UL) would rate
products and companies would pay to have its seal placed on their
products. Two, consumers would pay to find out ratings on a subscription
basis or by product. (sort of consumer reports, but better.)

In answer to obvious questions like companies bribing services to
give them a false rating, it is clear the same can happen with the
government but they have a monopoly and this system would have
competitors. Any service foolish enough to accept bribes and discovered
would soon be out of business. C'est le market!


I think this would be a good supplement to the FAQ since it would
answer some of the repetitive debate and would not stifle legit
challenges. Extropians should be so dogma bound as to not be willing
to listen to new challenges. If the ideas are sound, criticism and
testing can only serve to improve them.

Andrew Hall

