Date: Thu, 11 Feb 93 09:32:23 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Economic benefits of liberty

Not only does government produce goods and services at twice the
cost of the free market; in a market where it is the only customer
it BUYS goods and services at twice the commercial cost.  My empirical
data comes from a competition Boeing was involved with to supply the
Air Force with rockets to deliver the GPS navigation satellites.
We also priced the same vehicle as an internally funded program to
deliver communications satellites.  The difference amounted to a
factor of 2 in cost, mostly due to paperwork overhead, expected change
orders, and being required to follow outmoded design practices
(specified in Military Standards).

The mind also boggles at the pile of paper being accumulated in
the current 'Critical Design Review' for the Space Station hardware
Boeing is building.  The 10,000,000 pages easily exceeds the
weight of the actual hardware (150,000 lb) by a significant factor
(400,000 lb paper).  For those unaware of what a CDR is, it occurs
when 75-90% of the drawings are complete, i.e. when most of the
detail design is complete.  NASA will do a thorough review of all
our design information, and will provide constructive criticism
where it thinks something needs changing.  Given satisfactory
resolution of the review comments, we then get the go-ahead to
build the hardware.  The CDR is not a single thing, there is a
review for each part of the hardware.  The bulk of the reviews
will occur in the next three months.

For us, it is a major turning point, going from design to
construction.  As inefficient as the NASA process for getting a
Space Station has been, at least we are getting to some real hardware.

Dani Eder

