I am still working through Robin's message about the analogy between
diversification in the stock market and in a betting market.  To help
me understand the point, I have a couple of questions:

1) Is the beta of a contingent asset, like one of Robin's Idea Futures,
always equal to the beta of the underlying asset (irrespective of any
relationship between the contingency and the asset)?

It _seems_ to me that the answer is no, but I'm not sure.  Assuming
the answer is no:

2) Is the average of the betas of two "opposite" contingent assets
(with the same underlying asset) always equal to the beta of the underlying
asset?

